## Type hierarchy

| Role | Desktop starting point | Narrow-screen starting point |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Page heading | 48px, bold, 1.1 line height | 36px, bold |
| Section heading | 30px, bold, 1.2 line height | 24px, bold |
| Card heading | 24px, bold, 1.25 line height | 20px to 24px |
| Body and main controls | 19px, regular, 1.5 line height | 18px to 19px |
| Supporting metadata | 16px, regular, 1.5 line height | 16px |
| Primary sensor value | 32px to 48px, tabular numerals where useful | Fit the value and unit without truncation |

These are starting points, not fixed values. Maintained custom civica UI uses 19px for body text and main controls, and 16px for metadata. Native Home Assistant dense controls and body text use 16px, supporting labels 14px and compact extra text 12px. Larger native headings keep their Home Assistant values, and card titles are 24px. This density adaptation protects native layout. Check it in live Home Assistant at 200% text size.

The shared custom base sizes are `civica-font-size-body` (19px), `civica-font-size-small` (16px) and `civica-font-size-heading` (24px). The native `ha-font-size-xs`, `ha-font-size-s`, `ha-font-size-m` and `ha-font-size-l` base sizes are 12, 14, 16 and 19px. Each value uses `calc()` with `--ha-font-size-scale`, so Home Assistant’s text scaling still works.

Native dashboard section titles in `hui-heading-card` use the 24px bold card-heading size, so a section title never looks weaker than the cards below it. The theme sets `ha-heading-card-title-font-size` to `var(--civica-font-size-heading)`, `ha-heading-card-title-font-weight` to `700` and `ha-heading-card-title-line-height` to `1.25`. This keeps Home Assistant’s text scale and heading structure. Subtitle and caption tokens keep their native supporting look.

## Make hierarchy semantic

Use one page heading and keep heading levels in order. A large sensor number such as “21 °C” is a value, not automatically a heading. Do not style a paragraph to look like a heading while leaving it as a paragraph.

Use sentence case. Use bold for labels and selective emphasis, not whole paragraphs. Avoid uppercase button labels, italics for essential instructions and pale grey text that lowers contrast.

## Keep text readable

Keep instructions next to their controls and let labels wrap. Use an ellipsis only for non-essential overflow, and only where the full value is available without hovering. A unit, warning or room name needed to identify a device must always be visible.

Allow browser text resizing and the person’s font preferences. Do not disable zoom or give containers a fixed pixel height.
